<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Todor Zhivkov</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1911">1911</a>–<a href="/years/1998">1998</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Todor Zhivkov was the communist leader of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a> for 35 years, first secretary of the Bulgarian Communist Party from 1954 to 1989 and head of state from 1971. He kept Bulgaria the most loyal Soviet ally in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-europe">Eastern Europe</a>, oversaw rapid industrialisation and in the 1980s led a forced assimilation campaign against the country's Turkish minority. He was ousted by reformers in his own party on 10 November 1989.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/todor-zhivkov.jpg" alt="Todor Zhivkov" width="320" height="451" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Extracted by Fredy.00 · CC BY-SA 3.0 cz · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Todor_Jivkov.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Todor Hristov Zhivkov was born in the village of Pravets into a peasant family. The exact date of his birth was disputed within the family: his mother insisted on 20 September 1911, but in his memoirs he said that a drunken <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a> priest had recorded only the day of baptism, and calculated from local custom that the real day was 7 September.</p><p>During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> he took part in the resistance and in 1943 helped organise the Chavdar Partisan Brigade around his birthplace. After the uprising of 9 September 1944 he became head of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sofia">Sofia</a> police, restyled the People's Militia, and he rose in the Bulgarian Communist Party, joining its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/politburo">Politburo</a> in 1951 and helping counter rural resistance to forced collectivisation.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p>Zhivkov became First Secretary in 1954, replacing the hardline Valko Chervenkov, who kept most of his powers as prime minister. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nikita-khrushchev">Nikita Khrushchev</a>'s secret speech against Stalin, the April plenum of 1956 saw Zhivkov criticise Chervenkov, who was demoted, and he became the de facto leader. He served as prime minister from 1962 to 1971 and then as Chairman of the State Council. His government carried out a relative liberalisation and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/de-stalinization">de-Stalinization</a>, removed monuments of personality cults, allowed some market reforms and ended the persecution of the Bulgarian Orthodox Church. In 1965 a plot by officers who wanted a pro-Chinese Stalinist-Maoist leadership was exposed.</p><p>He followed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> closely, supporting the invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czechoslovakia">Czechoslovakia</a> in 1968, though he also drew advantage from the relationship, notably in Soviet crude oil refined at Burgas and resold. The economy grew from the mid-1960s to the late 1970s, with new industrial plants and the Kozloduy nuclear power station. Dissidents could still be punished, and exit visas were needed to travel abroad.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In December 1984 the government began the Revival Process, a campaign to replace Turkish with Bulgarian names that many Bulgarian Turks resisted and that led to a large-scale expulsion of Turks and Muslims in 1989. A stagnating economy, a worsening international image and corruption in the party weakened him, and under pressure from senior party members he resigned on 10 November 1989. Communist rule effectively ended within a month.</p><p>He was tried on five indictments, including the Revival Process and labour camps, was later released, and wrote memoirs. He died on 5 August 1998. Popularly nicknamed bai Tosho, he remains the longest-serving non-royal ruler in Bulgarian history.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Todor Zhivkov” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After <b>Todor Zhivkov's</b> fall in November 1989 ended the dominance of the People's Republic of Bulgaria's communist party, Round Table talks led to free elections in 1990.</blockquote><a href="/state/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a></li><li><blockquote>Under <b>Todor Zhivkov</b>, party leader from 1954, Bulgaria became the most faithful Soviet ally, industrialising rapidly with Soviet energy and markets.</blockquote><a href="/state/peoples-republic-of-bulgaria">People's Republic of Bulgaria</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Pravets, in what is now <a href="/countries/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sofia">Sofia</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=42.89,23.92&amp;year=1967" aria-label="Pravets on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5128 640 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Todor Zhivkov"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5578 910h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5578 910h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=42.89,23.92&amp;year=1967">What was here in 1967? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
